
<p>Mike McIntire and Eli Murray, reporting for The New York Times (gift link)</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>For nearly two decades, judges across the country have invoked
this language to strike down gun-control laws. It is a pivotal
legal test.</p>

<p>It is also based on a misquote.</p>

<p>Justice Antonin Scalia, <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2008/06/27/washington/27scotus.html">writing for the majority</a> in the 2008
case, District of Columbia v. Heller, attributed the “dangerous
and unusual” language to Sir William Blackstone, an 18th-century
English jurist whose writings became the basis for much of
American law.</p>

<p>But in his seminal 1769 treatise, “Commentaries on the Laws of
England,” Blackstone referred to weapons that were “<a href="https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_commentaries-on-the-laws_blackstone-william-sir_1775_4/page/148/mode/2up?q=unusual">dangerous
<em>or</em> unusual</a>,” suggesting that a gun did not need to be both
in order to be restricted.</p>
</blockquote>

<p><a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2018/02/16/nra-2nd-amendment">I’ve written before</a> that <em>Heller</em> overturned two centuries of Supreme Court interpretation of the 2nd Amendment. The current interpretation — literally the NRA’s interpretation — was <a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2018/03/27/stevens-2nd-amemdment">characterized by former Chief Justice Warren Burger</a> as “one of the greatest pieces of fraud, I repeat the word fraud, on the American public by special interest groups that I have ever seen in my lifetime.”</p>

<p>So it’s not like anything needs to rest on this and-vs.-or misquoting of something William Blackstone wrote before the United States was even founded. But, still, it’s interesting nonetheless how often the gun-fetish jurists like to cite the misquoting. The government should, and can, regulate dangerous weapons.</p>

